  "id": "theweave",
  "neon": "#9fe0ff",
  "note": "THE WEAVE — the Ascendants’ body shop: a twisting chrome-banded works tower on the foundry floor\nit is named for. It extrudes chrome, and it is built to look like what it makes.\n\n⚠ THE STACK OF CROSSED SLABS IS GONE, AND THAT IS A REPLACEMENT RATHER THAN A RESTYLE. Four long\nslabs rotated against each other is a real idea and it read as a pile of boxes from every bearing:\na weave is corners you cannot follow, and stacked rectangles give you eight hard ones per slab. A\ncontinuous twist has none.\n\n⚠ THE SHAFT IS A CURTAIN WALL, NOT A BILLET, and that is the difference between a building and a\nsculpture. Each of seven storeys is a glazed band with a chrome spandrel RECESSED 0.024 behind it,\nso the tower carries a floor rhythm and a real shadow line at every level. It had to be done in\nGEOMETRY because a yawed plate can carry no bolted-on part at all — see the anchoring note below —\nand inset segments are the one kind of articulation that survives the twist.\n\n⚠ THE SECTION IS SQUARE, AND THAT IS CORRECTNESS RATHER THAN TASTE. `helixRunner` traces a CIRCLE\nof radius r0..r1, so it lands on a corner only where the corner sits at ONE radius — true of a\nsquare plate (hw·root2), false of every rectangle. The slabs this replaces were 0.42 by 0.15 and\ntheir runner hung in open air for most of its height; the Gate’s piers made the same mistake at\n0.128 by 0.295. A square plate makes it exact. All FOUR corners are run here, against the Spire’s\ntwo: that tower is tall enough that a corner is always in view and this one is not.\n\n⚠ IT HAS TO READ AS A PLACE SOMEBODY WORKS. Every vision band keeps a window grid, so it has\nfloors and lights itself after dark. There is a foundry podium with the works on show behind blast\nglass, a canopy, a lit name band and louvred plant; a SERVICE SIDE at the back with a roller\nshutter, a condenser and its drip; and a PLANT FLOOR under the crown, which every real tower has.\n\n⚠ EVERY BOLTED-ON PART IS ON AN UNYAWED BOX. `anchored` takes its wall planes from unyawed boxes\nonly, so a plate inside the twist can carry nothing — which is exactly why the plant floor is\nauthored square and why the whole street kit is on the podium.\n\n⚠ THE SHAFT IS `ty_asc_shaft` AND THE BANDS ARE `ty_asc_chrome`, THE SPIRE’S OWN SPLIT, AND IT IS\nNOT NEGOTIABLE. CHROME_WALL is a flat clad panel with no coursing and no window grid, so a whole\nshaft in it is an unlit obelisk every night — thespire.json records exactly that about its own\ntower. The mirror goes where there are no windows to lose: the podium collar, the spandrels, the\nplant floor and the cap. Lighting sits on the shaft the bands interrupt, never on the bands,\nbecause a mirror with nothing behind it is dark.\n\n⚠ THE TWIST IS 1.90 RAD OVER 0.75 OF THE HEIGHT, against the Spire’s 1.80 over 2.60 — about three\ntimes the rate. Two buildings on one campus sharing a move and disagreeing about the rate read as\nrelated; the same rate would read as a copy.\n⚠ A PANE IS THE PART’S OWN `glass` COLOUR AND NEVER A PALETTE KEY. A `ty_weave_glass` was added\nto windshield.js for this and taken straight back out: `windowBay` takes its surround from `pal`\nand its pane from `glass`, so the key had no reader at all.\nRENDER_TUNE.legacyArms puts the asc_weave arm back.",
  "pal": "ty_asc_weave",
